# Network Intrusion Detection: ML vs Deep Learning on CIC-IDS2017

Compares 3 machine learning and 3 deep learning models for detecting network attacks on the CIC-IDS2017 dataset (15 traffic classes, 78 flow features). All models train on the same preprocessed data and are scored on the same untouched test set, so the comparison is fair.

## Results

| Model | Accuracy | Precision | Recall | F1 (macro) | MCC | Train time (s) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **XGBoost** | 0.9985 | 0.756 | 0.771 | **0.763** | 0.995 | 426 |
| LightGBM | 0.9983 | 0.759 | 0.766 | 0.762 | 0.995 | 276 |
| Random Forest | 0.9976 | 0.725 | 0.755 | 0.734 | 0.992 | 85 |
| MLP | 0.9493 | 0.521 | 0.839 | 0.591 | 0.867 | 155 |
| Autoencoder + MLP | 0.9374 | 0.463 | 0.837 | 0.548 | 0.841 | 223 |
| 1D CNN | 0.9109 | 0.380 | 0.756 | 0.438 | 0.780 | 425 |

Tree-based models clearly beat the neural networks on this tabular flow data. The MLP-style models catch more attacks (higher recall) but raise many more false alarms (lower precision).

## Pipeline

1. Load the daily CSVs (10% sample each), then remove duplicates, infinities and missing values.
2. Encode labels and make an 80/20 stratified train/test split.
3. Fit StandardScaler on training data only (no leakage).
4. Apply SMOTE to the training set only, raising rare classes to 30% of the majority class.
5. Train all six models on the same resampled training set.
6. Evaluate every model with one shared `evaluate()` function, then explain the best models with SHAP.

## Project structure

```
00_Preprocessing.ipynb    load, clean, EDA, encode, split, scale, SMOTE
01_RandomForest.ipynb     02_XGBoost.ipynb     03_LightGBM.ipynb
04_MLP.ipynb              05_CNN1D.ipynb       06_AutoencoderMLP.ipynb
07_Comparison.ipynb       comparison table, charts, SHAP
utils.py                  shared evaluate() and save/load helpers
results/                  per-model metrics (JSON) and comparison_table.csv
plots/                    confusion matrices and comparison charts
docs/index.html           results website (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, Chart.js)
```

## How to run

1. Download the dataset from https://www.unb.ca/cic/datasets/ids-2017.html and put the CSV files in a `Data/` folder.
2. Install dependencies: `pip install -r requirements.txt`
3. Run `00_Preprocessing.ipynb` first, then notebooks `01` to `06` in any order, then `07_Comparison.ipynb`.

## Tech stack

- **Languages:** Python, JavaScript, HTML, CSS
- **ML and data:** scikit-learn, XGBoost, LightGBM, TensorFlow/Keras, imbalanced-learn (SMOTE), SHAP, pandas, NumPy
- **Visualization:** matplotlib, seaborn, Chart.js
- **Tools:** Jupyter Notebook, Git, GitHub Pages

## Limitations

- Only a 10% sample of the dataset was used; very rare classes (for example Heartbleed, Infiltration) have too few rows to judge reliably.
- Results come from a single train/test split.
- ROC-AUC is not reported yet.

## Dataset

CIC-IDS2017, Canadian Institute for Cybersecurity, University of New Brunswick.
